[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Scenes, GameObjects, and Components Lifecycle in Unity"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unity development relies heavily on understanding how core architectural elements interact and persist through time. At the foundation are Scenes, GameObjects, and Components, each governed by a specific execution lifecycle."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Scenes and Scene Management"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Scene contains the environments and menus of your game. Think of each unique Scene file as a unique level. In Unity, you can load and unload scenes synchronously or asynchronously using the SceneManagement namespace."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"using UnityEngine;
using UnityEngine.SceneManagement;

public class SceneLoader : MonoBehaviour
{
    public void LoadNextLevel()
    {
        SceneManager.LoadScene("GameScene");
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. GameObjects and Components"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"GameObjects are the fundamental containers in Unity, but they do nothing on their own. They act as containers for Components. The behavior, rendering, physics, and scripting of a GameObject are entirely driven by the attached Components."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"GameObject: The entity existing within a Scene hierarchy."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Component: The functional scripts, colliders, renderers, and rigidbodies attached to GameObjects."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"3. The Execution Lifecycle Order"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Every active script derived from MonoBehaviour follows a strict chronological event execution order. Understanding this lifecycle prevents common null reference exceptions and logic bugs."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Awake(): Called when the script instance is being loaded. Always used for initialization variables or state before the game starts."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Start(): Called only if the script component is enabled, just before any Update method is called for the first time."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Update(): Called once per frame. Used for regular game logic, input collection, and movement."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"FixedUpdate(): Called at a fixed time interval. Essential for Rigidbody physics calculations."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"OnDestroy(): Called when the GameObject or Scene is destroyed, ideal for cleaning up events or memory."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"By mastering Scenes, GameObjects, and the MonoBehaviour lifecycle, you build scalable, predictable, and high-performance Unity applications."}]
